('kind:gh-2913', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2913", "title": "[feat] Python code extraction", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nCodeAgents become more popular where LLM generates Python code that later executed and result extracted from the code run rather that directly from the LLM output.\n\nJust like with JSON, Python is a formalized language that requires certain rules to be followed by LLM for the code being in a valid and executable form.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nI''d like BAML to facilitate Python code generation, extraction and validation similar to JSON.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nCurrently we extract code from LLM output try to compile and execute to find out if it’s valid.\n\nI believe BAML could reside between LLM and the code execution/compiling to provide a better accuracy and quality .\n\n### Additional Context\n\nRelates to https://github.com/pydantic/pydantic/issues/12671", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2799', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2799", "title": "[feat] Java library", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nBAML provides a lot of functionality that is very useful to anyone interested in interacting with one or more LLM providers. Many of the features are not available to languages without a proper client library.\n\nJava is a [popular programming language](https://survey.stackoverflow.co/2025/technology#most-popular-technologies-language) used by many organizations. The lack of a java library means that a proxy server (either the BAML server, or a language with a covered library utilizing the BAML client) is required.\n\nThis adds a deployment dependency to any application, and reduces adoption of BAML.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nI would like a BAML generator for java, as well as a client which has access to the core functionality of BAML, including parsing responses.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2781', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2781", "title": "[feat] AWS bedrock client support for guardrails", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nThe current client LLM API works well with AWS credentials. However, they do not support a way to pass guardrails information like identifier and version to pass through guardrail checks for AWS requests. The modular API can be an alternative but there is no support for streaming. If baml can natively support the passing guardrail information through client llm that would be a great feature special for the organizations who enabled guardrails as their protection layer for llm requests/response. I don''t want to miss the value baml brings in this AI world because of this small issue. Appreciate if you can add this feature at your earliest.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\n client<llm> CustomBedrock {\n   provider aws-bedrock\n    options {\n       model \"anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-20250514-v1:0\"\n       region \"us-east-1\"\n       **guardrail_identifier \"abcdefghijk\"**\n       **guardrail_version  \"DRAFT\"**\n   }\n }\n\nAlternatively we can also pass it to the baml function using baml_options\nb.ExtractResume(\"Vaibhav Gupta ....\",  **baml_options = {\"guardrail_identifier\":\"abcdefghijk\" , \"guardrail_version\": \"DRAFT\"}**)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2777', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2777", "title": "[feat] explicit support for convex", "body": "We''ve had a few users ask questions about how to do something with it now:\n\n* [https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1407153907549081651](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1407153907549081651)\n* [https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1253172325205934181/1380974719876071474](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1253172325205934181/1380974719876071474)\n* [https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1418854001641783366](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1418854001641783366)", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2746', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2746", "title": "[feat] Image output", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nPretty simple. Common to want a image or multi-modal model to generate an image based on text and/or another image, and there''s currently no way to get that out. This is just a design exercise for what the DX of using that might look like at the language level.\n\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\n\n# Proposal 0\n\nThis is the simplest, but doesn''t allow you get anything but the image out of results. Probably good enough for a lot of use cases.\n\n```\nenum PhotoCategory {\n  Photo\n  Diagram\n  Cartoon\n}\n\n// This version takes the first image found and returns it, ignoring any others\nfunction GenerateImage(itemDesc: string, category: PhotoCategory) -> Image {\n  Please generate an image.\n\n  Description: {{ itemDesc }}\n  \n  Category: {{ category }}\n}\n\n// Or, since some models support multiple returns \nfunction GenerateImages(itemDesc: string[], category: PhotoCategory) -> Image[] {\n  prompt #\" \n  Please generate an image for each description.\n\n  Descriptions: {{ itemDesc }}\n  \n  Category: {{ category }}\n  \"#\n}\n\n```\n\n# Proposal 1\n\nSupporting this would be pretty ideal, but requires you release the tuple type into the wild.\n\n```\n\nenum PhotoCategory {\n  Photo\n  Diagram\n  Cartoon\n}\n\n// This is probably a bad idea with current models\nfunction DetectAndConvertPhoto(photo: Image, category: PhotoCategory) -> tuple<PhotoCategory, Image>\n  prompt #\"\n     Please convert this image to {{ category }} and tell me what category it was before.\n  \"#\n}\n\n```\n\n# Proposal 2\n\nIntegrate images into the output structure. This gives you ability to describe your semantic output. Probably my favorite.\n\n```\nenum PhotoCategory {\n  Photo\n  Diagram\n  Cartoon\n}\n\nclass WasPhoto {\n  light image @description(\"Version with a white background\")\n  dark image @description(\"Version with a dark background\")\n  category PhotoCategory\n}\n\n// This is probably a bad idea with current models\nfunction DetectAndConvertPhoto(photo: Image, category: PhotoCategory) -> WasPhoto\n  prompt #\"\n     Please convert this image to {{ category }} and tell me what category it was before. Generate\n     two versions, one light mode and one dark mode.\n  \"#\n}\n```\n\n\nWhen describing the output format, it would have to be something like this:\n```\n   WasPhoto:\n    - light: first image generated\n    - dark: second image generated\n    - category: Photo, Diagram, Cartoon\n```\n\nAnd then basically as you loop through the parts of the response, or however each individual model works, you assign them in", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2717', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2717", "title": "[feat] TypeBuilder API and Collector for REST client", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nAs of now REST client does not support TypeBuilder API and Collector.\n\nFor use case like getting reasoning from llm collector is required.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nSupport for TypeBuilder API and Collector in REST client.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n*No response*\n\n### Additional Context\n\nBackground: [https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1410654071869931693](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1410654071869931693)", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
