('kind:gh-2609', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2609", "title": "[feat] # Proposal: LLM-Friendly Flat Schemas & Reusable Modeling for BAML", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nBuilding structured outputs for **multi-type × multi-difficulty** Q&A on synthesis with LLM  (e.g., chemistry items across single/multi/open/calculation/hybrid and easy/medium/hard) currently causes two classes of issues:\n\n1. **LLM-unfriendly shape**\n\n* Deeply nested schemas (e.g., `output.items.easy[0].answer.payload.option`) increase LLM formatting errors: missing fields, misplaced values, and inconsistent variants.\n* The model must “remember” and traverse multiple layers, which raises hallucination risk when filling JSON.\n\n2. **Engineer-unfriendly duplication**\n\n* Separate `QA / Section / Output` classes for each question type lead to copy-paste boilerplate.\n* Cross-cutting changes (e.g., adding a field to `QuestionInfo`) require editing many near-identical types, inviting drift and bugs.\n\n**Goal**: Expose a **flat, unambiguous** structure to the LLM (to improve stability), while giving engineers **reusable, type-safe** tools (to reduce boilerplate). All improvements should be **compile-time only** with **zero runtime cost**.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nImplement three compile-time features/patterns and use them in an end-to-end example:\n\n### A) Discriminated union + `@flatten` (model-facing, for stability)\n\n* **`oneof … @discriminant(\"kind\")`** defines mutually exclusive variants with a single tag field `kind`.\n* **`@flatten`** lifts a member’s fields into the host at the **serialization/LLM interface**, removing one nesting level.\n* **Compile-time consistency checks**: If `kind = single`, only `option` is allowed; conflicting fields are errors with source locations.\n\n**Minimal BAML (model-facing types)**\n\n```baml\nenum AnswerKind { single multi open calculation hybrid }\n\nclass OptionsMap { A string  B string  C string  D string }\n\nclass QuestionInfo {\n  question string\n  options OptionsMap?              @description(\"Only for choice questions\")\n  difficulty DifficultyEnum\n  why string\n}\n\nclass CalcuAnswer {\n  value float\n  unit string\n  formula string\n  conclusion string\n}\n\noneof AnswerPayload @discriminant(\"kind\") {\n  single      { option  OptionEnum }\n  multi       { options OptionEnum[] }\n  open        { text    string }\n  calculation CalcuAnswer\n  hybrid      { calc CalcuAnswer  text string? }\n}\n\nclass QA {\n  question_info QuestionInfo\n  payload AnswerPayload @flatten   @description(\"Expose `kind` + variant fields at top level\")\n}\n```\n\n**What the LLM sees (flat, ≤2 main levels)**\n\n*Single choice*\n\n``", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2592', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2592", "title": "[feat] Date / DateTime support", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\n\"I''d love to bring back the request for date/datetime support. We have a lot of weird code to handle the passing of strings that should be dates between frontend/backend/database\"\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nn/a\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2417', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2417", "title": "[feat] template_string in attributes", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\n[https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1411116748983238737](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1411116748983238737)\n\n1. \n   > ### **3:34 PM*\\]*ChadGPT***:*\n   >\n   > Can you use template_string in description?\n2. \n   > ### ***\\[*****4:06 PM*\\]*sam***:*\n   >\n   > Not at the moment! Can I ask what the use case looks like? Trying to get a sense of if there are other language features that might help more in this case\n3. \n   > ### ***\\[*****6:31 PM*\\]*ChadGPT***:*\n   >\n   > Hi Sam, sorry I completely missed this message. But I have multiple class that have similar fields with multiline description, the examples are super made up but something like\n   >\n   > ```\n   > class Pineapple {\n   >   sweetness_effects string[] @description(#\"\n   >   How sweet the pineapple is in concise natural language:\n   > \n   >   Use an array to represent tier of sweetness and their effects:\n   >   Tier 1:\n   >   Properties found in common instances of the item\n   >   Tier 2:\n   >   Special properties only available in the rarest instances\n   >   ...\n   >   \"#)\n   > }\n   > \n   > class Computer {\n   >   power_consumption string[] @description(#\"\n   >   How much power does a compute unit consumes in verbose natural language:\n   > \n   >   Use an array to represent tier of sweetness and their effects:\n   >   Tier 1:\n   >   Properties found in common instances of the item\n   >   Tier 2:\n   >   Special properties only available in the rarest instances\n   >   ...\n   >   \"#)\n   > }\n   > ```", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["customer", "feature (big)"]}'::jsonb, 'customer,feature (big)'),
('kind:gh-2912', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2912", "title": "[feat] Support accepting `AbortSignal` via the Next.js plugin hooks", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nI want to be able to [cancel requests](https://docs.boundaryml.com/guide/baml-basics/abort-signal).\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\n```ts\nimport { useWriteMeAStory } from \"@/baml_client/react/hooks\";\n\nconst hook = useWriteMeAStory({\n  signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000),\n});\n\n// or\nhook.mutate(..., { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });\n```\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2882', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2882", "title": "[bug] Include `@description` value in Pydantic type", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nText written inside of `@description` is not included in the Pydantic type generated by baml. Therefore when using `model_json_schema()` the generated schema doesn''t contain the description.\n\nThis is how types are currently generated:\n```py\nclass Resume(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    email: str\n    experience: typing.List[str]\n    skills: typing.List[str]\n```\n```py\nResume.model_json_schema()\n# {\"properties\": {\"name\": {\"title\": \"Name\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"email\": {\"title\": \"Email\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"experience\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"title\": \"Experience\", \"type\": \"array\"}, \"skills\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"title\": \"Skills\", \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"name\", \"email\", \"experience\", \"skills\"], \"title\": \"Resume\", \"type\": \"object\"}\n```\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nRun `baml-cli init`, `baml-cli generate` and run\n```py\nfrom baml_client.types import Resume\nprint(Resume.model_json_schema())\n```\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.215.2\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nPython\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2879', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2879", "title": "[feat] turbopack compatibility in baml-nextjs-plugin", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nWhen running `next dev --turbopack`, the BAML Next.js plugin fails to load properly. Since Nextjs is committed to turbopack in their roadmap, in the future BAML won''t be able to claim first class Nextjs support without turbopack compatibility.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nEnable turbopack (king move)\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\nUse webpack (boring)\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2849', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2849", "title": "Feature Request: AWS Web Identity Token / IRSA support for aws-bedrock", "body": "## Summary\n\nAdd support for AWS Web Identity Token authentication (IRSA - IAM Roles for Service Accounts) to the `aws-bedrock` provider.\n\n## Related Issue\n\nThis is the AWS equivalent of #2560 (Workload Identity Federation for Vertex AI).\n\n## Problem\n\nWhen running BAML in Amazon EKS with IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA), the `aws-bedrock` provider cannot authenticate automatically.\n\nIRSA provides credentials via:\n- `AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE` - path to the projected service account token\n- `AWS_ROLE_ARN` - the IAM role to assume\n\nBut the `aws-bedrock` provider only reads:\n- `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID` / `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` / `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN`\n- `AWS_PROFILE`\n\nIRSA is the standard authentication method for production EKS workloads calling AWS services. Without native support, users must implement manual credential exchange.\n\n## Current Workaround\n\nWe use `@aws-sdk/credential-providers`''s `fromTokenFile()` to manually exchange the web identity token for temporary credentials, then pass them explicitly to BAML''s `ClientRegistry`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { fromTokenFile } from ''@aws-sdk/credential-providers'';\n\nasync function getIRSACredentials() {\n  const credentialsProvider = fromTokenFile(); // reads AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE + AWS_ROLE_ARN\n  const credentials = await credentialsProvider();\n  return {\n    accessKeyId: credentials.accessKeyId,\n    secretAccessKey: credentials.secretAccessKey,\n    sessionToken: credentials.sessionToken,\n  };\n}\n\n// Then pass to ClientRegistry\nconst creds = await getIRSACredentials();\ncr.addLlmClient(''bedrock'', ''aws-bedrock'', {\n  model: ''us.meta.llama3-3-70b-instruct-v1:0'',\n  region: ''us-west-2'',\n  access_key_id: creds.accessKeyId,\n  secret_access_key: creds.secretAccessKey,\n  session_token: creds.sessionToken,\n});\n```\n\nThis works but adds complexity and requires caching/refreshing credentials manually.\n\n## Suggested Implementation\n\nThe Rust [`aws-config`](https://docs.rs/aws-config/latest/aws_config/) crate''s `DefaultCredentialsChain` already includes `WebIdentityTokenCredentialsProvider`. Using this for credential resolution (instead of only checking explicit env vars) would enable IRSA automatically.\n\nAlternatively, a minimal implementation could:\n1. Check for `AWS_WEB_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE` + `AWS_ROLE_ARN` env vars\n2. Call STS `AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity` to exchange the token\n3. Use the returned temporary credentials\n\n## Use Case\n\nProduction Kubernetes (EKS) deployment using BAML to call Amazon Bedrock for LLM inference.", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2840', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2840", "title": "[feat] expose full model name via Collector API", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nI need to access the actual LLM model name for analytics. The `Collector` does not surface it, so we currently infer it by parsing provider-specific HTTP request metadata. This is brittle and provider-dependent.\n\n**Current workaround**\n\n```ts\nconst log = collector.last\n// Use calls array directly (properly typed) since selectedCall is typed as unknown\nconst llmCall = log?.calls?.find((c) => c.selected) ?? log?.calls?.at(-1)\nconst latencyMs = log?.timing?.durationMs ?? 0\nconst latencySeconds = latencyMs / 1000\nconst usage = log?.usage\nconst inputTokens = usage?.inputTokens ?? null\nconst outputTokens = usage?.outputTokens ?? null\n// Extract actual model name from HTTP request\n// - OpenAI-style: model in request body\n// - Google AI: model in URL path (/models/{model}:generateContent)\nconst requestBody = llmCall?.httpRequest?.body?.json() as { model?: string } | undefined\nconst urlModelMatch = llmCall?.httpRequest?.url?.match(/\\/models\\/([^/:]+)/)\nconst aiModel = requestBody?.model ?? urlModelMatch?.[1] ?? llmCall?.clientName ?? \"unknown\"\nconst aiProvider = llmCall?.provider ?? \"unknown\"\n```\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\n- Add a normalized, typed `model` field to each `Call` (and/or `selectedCall`) in the `Collector` logs.\n  - Example: `collector.last.calls[i].model: string`\n- Ensure `provider` remains reliably available and typed.\n- Optionally, type `selectedCall` concretely so consumers don’t need to re-scan `calls`.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\nWe had to do this because we wanted to send the information to PostHog for LLM analytics", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2797', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2797", "title": "[feat] Shortcut for commenting out lines in .baml prompts", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nWhen I''m testing prompts, I find myself commenting out certain lines of the prompt (which may be single lines or prompt template placeholders) and then observing the result in the playground. The Jinja comment template is \"{# #}\" but there isn''t a quick shortcut to comment it out. The default for .baml files is \"//\". My options today are either to manually add those Jinja comment braces in or delete that line.\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nAllow shortcut like Ctrl + / to comment out lines in BAML prompts per Jinja syntax {# #} rather than //\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2794', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2794", "title": "[feat] Ctrl+F search in the IDE Playground", "body": "### Product\n\nVS Code Playground\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nMy prompts are fairly large and I love the IDE Playground to view the raw request being sent to the LLM API as well as running the playground. However, I also use a lot of prompt templating so my .baml file is a lot of placeholders and conditions. \n\nIf I want to verify a particular term or line exists in my prompt, there''s no easy way to do that. I would have loved to just do a search on the Playground where the full raw request is shown but Ctrl + F doesn''t work (tried it in Cursor and VS Code).\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nAllow for Ctrl + F in the Playground prompt so one can search within the prompt\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2780', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2780", "title": "[docs] collector interface for streaming calls needs work", "body": "`collector.usage.cachedInputTokens` is `null` when used with streamed anthropic via bedrock, and we also don''t document `.sse_responses()`\n\nhttps://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1447360451041300621", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2719', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2719", "title": "[feat] gemini context caching", "body": "From codager on discord:\n\n> ### ***\\[*****9:22 PM*\\]*** **OP** **codager***:*\n>\n> I have 2 use cases\n>\n> 1. I want to cache the system prompt across multiple invocations\n> 2. I want to upload the file to gemini and use that across prompts to get token benefits.\n>\n>  Are these possible through BAML?", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-2673', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2673", "title": "[feat] allow using claude code as a client<llm>", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nMake a locally installed and configured (and logged in) claude code cli available as a baml client\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nAdd claude code as a new client type within the runtime and ensure that client options within baml covering a sane set of CLI options for claude\ncode.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\nI saw #2229 but it was closed without response, I hope this sheds more light on the request. I could also imagine codex support the same way.", "kind": "feature", "labels": ["feature (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'feature (small)'),
('kind:gh-4869', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-4869", "title": "[bug] sdkgen_rust 0.19.0 exits 0 after skipping user functions, yielding a partial client", "body": "## Product\n\nBAML (`output_type = \"rust\"`)\n\n## Describe the bug\n\nOn 0.19.0, the Rust generator can skip user-declared types and functions that it cannot represent, write a coherent-looking but partial client, print `Generated rust_client (...)`, and exit 0. A build pipeline that trusts the exit status and success-shaped summary cannot distinguish this from complete generation.\n\nThis is the remaining failure mode from #4370 after 0.19.0 fixed generation of functions whose implicit throws contract includes `ai.errors.Failure`. It is independent of that fixed throws-contract bug.\n\n## Reproduction steps\n\nCreate `baml.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"partial-client-probe\"\n\n[generator.rust_client]\noutput_type = \"rust\"\nnaming_convention = \"preserve-case\"\noutput_dir = \"crates\"\n```\n\nCreate `baml_src/main.baml`:\n\n```baml\nclient Probe = openai.GenericClient.new(model = \"m\", base_url = env.BASE_URL, api_key = env.API_KEY);\n\nclass Route {\n    command: \"graph.query\" | \"facts.query\"\n}\n\nfunction Vision(page: image) -> string {\n    client: Probe\n    prompt: `${page} Describe the image.`\n}\n\nfunction Query(text: string) -> Route {\n    client: Probe\n    prompt: `${text} ${ctx.output_format()}`\n}\n\nfunction Helper() -> string {\n    \"control\"\n}\n```\n\nRun:\n\n```console\n$ BAML_VERSION=0.19.0 baml generate\nwarning: skipped `user.Route`: field `command`: unsupported union arm: \"graph.query\"\nwarning: skipped `user.Query`: return: references skipped or unknown type `user.Route`\nwarning: skipped `user.Vision`: argument `page`: unsupported type: media (image)\n   Generated rust_client (128 file(s), 0 identifier renames ...)\n$ echo $?\n0\n```\n\nThe generated client contains only `Helper` and `Helper_async` among the three user functions (plus generated scaffolding). `Vision` provides a trigger independent of the dotted string-literal union; the `Route`/`Query` path also overlaps #4371.\n\n## Expected behavior\n\nIf generation skips any explicitly declared user type or function, `baml generate` should exit nonzero rather than report an ordinary successful generation. At minimum, the final summary should state prominently that the client is partial and how many user declarations were skipped.\n\n## Actual behavior\n\nGeneration exits 0 and prints the same success-shaped `Generated rust_client (...)` summary used for complete output. The only indication of missing API surface is a per-item warning, which is easy to miss among generator diagnostics.\n\n## Version and environment\n\n- BAML: published 0.19.0 GN", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug", "rust"]}'::jsonb, 'bug,rust'),
('kind:gh-2862', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2862", "title": "[bug] Go: `TypeBuilder#AddBaml` panics since v0.215.0", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen attempting to use `.AddBaml` on a `TypeBuilder` instance, BAML panics with the following error:\n\n```\nDecode: holder=&{NullValue:}\npanic: reflect: call of reflect.Value.Interface on zero Value\n\ngoroutine 1 [running]:\nreflect.valueInterface({0x0?, 0x0?, 0x102e26864?}, 0xc?)\n\t/opt/homebrew/Cellar/go/1.25.5/libexec/src/reflect/value.go:1478 +0xec\nreflect.Value.Interface(...)\n\t/opt/homebrew/Cellar/go/1.25.5/libexec/src/reflect/value.go:1473\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/raw_objects.decodeObjectResponse(0x14000012308?, 0x6?)\n\t/Users/<user>/go/pkg/mod/github.com/boundaryml/baml@v0.215.0/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/raw_objects/utils.go:184 +0x184\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/raw_objects.CallMethod({0x102f96d08, 0x14000078188}, {0x102e25072, 0x8}, 0x2710?)\n\t/Users/<user>/go/pkg/mod/github.com/boundaryml/baml@v0.215.0/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/raw_objects/utils.go:146 +0x2e4\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/pkg.(*typeBuilder).AddBaml(0x14000078188, {0x102e344b0, 0x27})\n\t/Users/<user>/go/pkg/mod/github.com/boundaryml/baml@v0.215.0/engine/language_client_go/pkg/rawobjects_type_builder.go:222 +0xec\ngithub.com/invakid404/baml-go-type-builder-issue/baml_client/type_builder.(*TypeBuilder).AddBaml(0x102f28660?, {0x102e344b0?, 0x102e24c63?})\n\t/Users/<user>/Coding/baml-go-type-builder-issue/baml_client/type_builder/type_builder.go:156 +0x28\nmain.main()\n\t/Users/<user>/Coding/baml-go-type-builder-issue/main.go:33 +0x204\nexit status 2\n```\n\nAlso note the `Decode:` log present in the output -- it appears 0.215.0 has debug logs of this variety left ungated in the release, they appear everywhere in the logs when calling BAML functions.\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nReproducible example: https://github.com/invakid404/baml-go-type-builder-issue\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.215.0\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nGo\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nmacOS\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2844', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2844", "title": "[bug] Prompt Fiddle`audio-input.baml` example broken", "body": "The `audio-input.baml` file cannot be run without encountering an error at the moment. I initially thought that this was because the keys weren''t configured, but I noticed that all other `.baml` files are working fine.\n\nSomething specific about the audio example is not working. See the video below:\n\nhttps://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c28b03de-bd54-4e9d-99e9-df283409952d\n\n@sxlijin", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2776', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2776", "title": "[bug] baml runtime hangs in specific Django/gunicorn context", "body": "> An update - I think we''ve figured out the root cause -> When gunicorn starts with preload_app = True, Django loads all modules - including our 23 baml_client packages - each of which calls BamlRuntime.from_files() and initializes its own Tokio async runtime, and after gunicorn forks the worker processes, the pre-initialized Tokio runtimes (with their thread pools, epoll file descriptors, and mutexes) become corrupted, causing the workers to hang due to the invalid internal state.\n\n[https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1446956944475820184](https://discord.com/channels/1119368998161752075/1446956944475820184)", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2677', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2677", "title": "promptfiddle shows error on first load", "body": "\"LLM client ''GPT4o'' requires environment variable ''OPENAI_API_KEY'' to be set but it is not\"", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2622', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2622", "title": "[bug] React hooks with streaming always return non-nullable types", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nFrom the docs:\n\n<img width=\"1405\" height=\"510\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/520878f6-62bf-4a87-9436-4470be906f97\" />\n\nBut the React hooks are always returning the non-nullable types.\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nGiven the following BAML:\n\n```baml\nclass MessageTest {\n  // MessageTest won''t stream until type is known\n  type \"error\" | \"success\" | \"info\" @stream.not_null\n\n  // MessageTest will only stream when title is known\n  title string @stream.done @stream.not_null\n\n  // Timestamp will only appear when fully complete, until then it will be null\n  timestamp string @stream.done\n\n  // will stream token by token, and include completion state\n  content string @stream.with_state\n\n  // will stream token by token\n  other string\n}\n\nfunction GetMessageTest(input: string) -> MessageTest {\n  client CustomGPT5\n  prompt #\"\n  \"#\n}\n```\n\nIt generates two types:\n\n```ts\n// types.ts\nexport interface MessageTest {\n  type: \"error\" | \"success\" | \"info\"\n  title: string\n  timestamp: string\n  content: string\n  other: string\n  \n}\n\n// partial_types.ts\n    interface MessageTest {\n      type: \"error\" | \"success\" | \"info\"\n      title: string\n      timestamp?: string | null\n      content?: StreamState<string | null>\n      other?: string | null\n    }\n```\n\nThen the hook''s return type is:\n\n```ts\n// const data: {\n//     type: \"error\" | \"success\" | \"info\";\n//     title: string;\n//     timestamp: string;\n//     content: string;\n//     other: string;\n// } | undefined\n// const data: MessageTest | undefined\n\nconst { data } = useGetMessageTest({ stream: true });\n```\n\nShouldn''t it be `partial_types.MessageTest`? Because it is actually returning null in the nullable fields, which is causing `Cannot access property on ...` bugs.\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.211.2\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nReact\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nmacOS\n\n### Browser\n\nFirefox\n\n### Code Editor\n\nVS Code", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2585', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2585", "title": "bug(vscode): tests keep hanging", "body": "Urgent for Ken: tests keep hanging — seems like a more common issue now\n\n[https://gloo-global.slack.com/archives/C06P60YREAC/p1759518106392139](https://gloo-global.slack.com/archives/C06P60YREAC/p1759518106392139)", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug", "bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug,bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2575', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2575", "title": "Initialize creating conflict for Datadog", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nHere''s the bug:\n\nanic: runtime error: invalid memory address or nil pointer dereference\n[signal SIGSEGV: segmentation violation code=0x1 addr=0x50 pc=0xf992ec]\n\ngoroutine 1 [running]:\ngithub.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/v2/instrumentation.(*Instrumentation).OperationName(0x20a71e0?, 0x4000112860?, 0x0)\n        /app/vendor/github.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/v2/instrumentation/instrumentation.go:79 +0x1c\ngithub.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/orchestrion.init.func5(0x4000438b40?)\n        /app/vendor/github.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/orchestrion/roundtrip.go:47 +0x2c\ngithub.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/wrap.ObserveRoundTrip(0x430eca0, 0x4000438b40)\n        /app/vendor/github.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/wrap/roundtrip.go:40 +0x6c\ngithub.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/orchestrion.ObserveRoundTrip(0x0?)\n        /app/vendor/github.com/DataDog/dd-trace-go/contrib/net/http/v2/internal/orchestrion/roundtrip.go:21 +0x28\nnet/http.(*Transport).RoundTrip(0x4318d80, 0x2b57be0?)\n        <generated>:7 +0x44\nnet/http.send(0x4000438b40, {0x2b57be0, 0x4318d80}, {0x7604b8?, 0x8?, 0x0?})\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:259 +0x498\nnet/http.(*Client).send(0x4e12680, 0x4000438b40, {0x4000586cc8?, 0x5994dc?, 0x0?})\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:180 +0x94\nnet/http.(*Client).do(0x4e12680, 0x4000438b40)\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:728 +0x764\nnet/http.(*Client).Do(...)\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:587\nnet/http.(*Client).Get(0x4e12680, {0x40001803f0?, 0xffff8fbe0f30?})\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:484 +0x74\nnet/http.Get(...)\n        /usr/local/go/src/net/http/client.go:453\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go.downloadChecksum({0x40001803f0, 0x6d}, {0x40003dc270, 0x29})\n        /app/vendor/github.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/lib.go:488 +0x5c\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go.downloadBamlLibrary({0x40003a9ba0, 0x1e}, {0x40003dc270, 0x29})\n        /app/vendor/github.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/lib.go:403 +0x2f4\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go.findOrDownloadLibrary()\n        /app/vendor/github.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go/lib.go:257 +0x614\ngithub.com/boundaryml/baml/engine/language_client_go/baml_go.initializeBaml(", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2441', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2441", "title": "[bug] Collector: when using fallback strategy with 2 clients , and if one of the call is failed, the selected_call will be populated with failed call.", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nlooks like if i use fallback strategy with 2 clients , and if one of the call is failed, the selected_call will be populated with failed call. is it expected? i am on 0.205.0\n\n<img width=\"319\" height=\"119\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b3660716-4705-4b68-9001-694f7d3972da\" />\n\n<img width=\"353\" height=\"121\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/008e4cf3-cf6e-4eef-8b0e-cb4a272bc8ac\" />\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\n1. create a client with fallback \n2. mark one client call failure (maybe by passing wrong key) and one success\n3. check the collector\n\n### BAML Version\n\n 0.205.0\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nPython\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nOpenAI\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug", "customer"]}'::jsonb, 'bug,customer'),
('kind:gh-2432', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2432", "title": "[bug] golang client: dynamic fields in classes not processed if class has no other fields", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nIf you define a class that is marked as `@@dynamic` that does not contain static fields, the generated Go client will never populate the `DynamicProperties` map.\n\nFor example:\n\n```baml\nclass SomeDynamicClass {\n  // no static fields\n  @@dynamic\n}\n```\n\nHere is what the generated `Decode` method for the corresponding Go struct in the generated client looks like:\n\n```go\nfunc (c *DynamicTestOutput) Decode(holder *cffi.CFFIValueClass, typeMap baml.TypeMap) {\n\ttypeName := holder.Name\n\tif typeName.Namespace != cffi.CFFITypeNamespace_TYPES {\n\t\tpanic(fmt.Sprintf(\"expected cffi.CFFITypeNamespace_TYPES, got %s\", string(typeName.Namespace.String())))\n\t}\n\tif typeName.Name != \"DynamicTestOutput\" {\n\t\tpanic(fmt.Sprintf(\"expected DynamicTestOutput, got %s\", typeName.Name))\n\t}\n\n   \n   c.DynamicProperties = make(map[string]any)\n   \n\n\t\n}\n```\n\nAs you may notice, `c.DynamicProperties` is constructed, but never mutated.\n\nLooking at [the template](https://github.com/BoundaryML/baml/blob/49f3b73d14f037642a2dd3f8e663098c20db4cc0/engine/generators/languages/go/src/_templates/class.go.j2#L26), the loop that decodes each individual field is generated iff the class has fields, and the check for whether it is dynamic is nested within it, meaning nothing gets generated in practice. Looks like special handling is required for cases where classes are dynamic and have no fields.\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\n1. Create a new BAML project configured to generate a Golang client\n2. Define some prompt whose output is a class marked as `@@dynamic` without any other fields\n3. Generate the client\n4. Observe the result\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.206.1\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nGo\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug", "bug (small)", "customer"]}'::jsonb, 'bug,bug (small),customer'),
('kind:gh-2413', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2413", "title": "[bug] React generated hooks issues with useEffect() and hooks dependencies", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen using the output of generated hooks as a dependency of another hook, you''ll end up with an infinite loop. Also it seems like something is not yet fully initialized if you try to mutate the function as soon as the generated hook is called.\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nInfinite loop example:\n```tsx\nexport function Component() {\n  const getMyFunction = useGetMyGeneratedFunction()\n  console.log(getMyFunction)\n\n  useEffect(() => {\n    getMyFunction.mutate(\"input\")\n  }, [getMyFunction.mutate])\n}\n```\n\nThe reason for this is the generated `useBamlAction` initialises a default `props` argument like so:\n```tsx\nfunction useBamlAction<FunctionName extends FunctionNames>(\n  action: ServerAction,\n  props: HookInput<FunctionName, { stream?: boolean }> = {},\n) {\n  ... \n  const mutate = useCallback(() => {\n    ...\n  }, [action, onFinalData, onError, props])\n}\n```\nTherefore the empty object is recreated at every call and the reference changes, making the dependency of mutate change.\n\nI removed  `props` from the `mutate` dependencies, to continue testing and then the streaming was working 50% of the time. In non streaming always works. When not working, what happened is the last response given by the stream would be my schema filled with null but then stops there. More precisely, at this line `const { value, done } = await reader.read()` of `useBamlAction`. In the server function side, the function would finish and logs the final result.\n\nSo I played a little bit to check for double calls (as suggested on your Discord) that React ([mainly in strict mode](https://react.dev/reference/react/StrictMode)) can do, but still no success. I tried to call `getMyFunction.mutate()` from a button and it would work every time. So I tried to delay the call in my `useEffect()` an indeed, then it worked. So here is what did work for me:\n\n```tsx\n  useEffect(() => {\n    if (tick === 0) {\n      setTimeout(() => {\n        setTick((prev) => prev + 1)\n      }, 1000)\n    }\n    if (tick === 1) {\n      useGetMyFunction.mutate(\"input\")\n    }\n  }, [tick, useGetMyFunction.mutate])\n```\n\n### Here is a list of suggestions regarding hooks:\n\nGenerated hooks currently looks like this:\n```tsx\nexport function useGetMyFunction(\n  props = {},\n) {\n  let action: ServerAction = Actions.GetMyFunction;\n  if (isStreamingProps(props)) {\n    action = StreamingActions.GetMyFunction;\n    return useBamlAction(action, props)\n  } else {\n    return useBamlAction(action, props)\n  }\n}\n``", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug", "customer"]}'::jsonb, 'bug,customer'),
('kind:gh-2385', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2385", "title": "[bug] VS code extension: Required files not found", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nHi,\n\nI upgraded today to the latest BAML extension for VS code, `0.206.0`.\n\nIt seems that the playground cannot be opened due to the following exception:\n```\nRequired files not found: Style file not found: /home/<user>/.vscode/extensions/boundary.baml-extension-0.206.0-linux-x64/dist/playground/dist/assets/index.css\n```\n\nHere''s the list of files in `assets`:\n```\nll /home/<user>/.vscode/extensions/boundary.baml-extension-0.206.0-linux-x64/dist/playground/dist/assets/\ntotal 28M\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  18M Aug 28 16:23 baml_schema_build-B1cfNhZF.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  42K Aug 28 16:23 bash-HNM5thJl.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 2.1K Aug 28 16:23 core-CI6ClEw0.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  74K Aug 28 16:23 core-CKLhzjyK.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  49K Aug 28 16:23 css-u4bm_LMz.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  15K Aug 28 16:23 github-dark-default-Cuk6v7N8.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  11K Aug 28 16:23 github-light-DAi9KRSo.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  58K Aug 28 16:23 html-DG1TtFnM.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 213K Aug 28 16:23 index-CqFN1MZt.css\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 7.1M Aug 28 16:23 index-OcowP5BG.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 181K Aug 28 16:23 javascript-DNgb01dh.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 2.9K Aug 28 16:23 json-DWmkr0yC.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 184K Aug 28 16:23 jsx-CM4fc1WH.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  56K Aug 28 16:23 markdown-CNXuF1yM.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  72K Aug 28 16:23 python-CK56Z9cH.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 885K Aug 28 16:23 ruby-DzsSxe0H.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  24K Aug 28 16:23 sql-D8nBeWSR.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 181K Aug 28 16:23 tsx-DqpEK-75.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 188K Aug 28 16:23 typescript-CVESyXxo.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 608K Aug 28 16:23 wasm-CG6Dc4jp.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  11K Aug 28 16:23 yaml-CIiI48o2.js\n```\n\nEverything seems to work with the previous version, 0.205.0. Here''s the list of assets for it:\n```\nll /home/<user>/.vscode/extensions/boundary.baml-extension-0.205.0-linux-x64/dist/playground/dist/assets/\ntotal 28M\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  18M Aug 28 16:31 baml_schema_build.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  42K Aug 28 16:31 bash.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  74K Aug 28 16:31 core2.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay 2.0K Aug 28 16:31 core.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  49K Aug 28 16:31 css.js\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nikolay nikolay  15K Aug 28 16:31 github-dark-default.js", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2335', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2335", "title": "[bug] pyi bindings are wrong", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen trying to create baml types by import `baml_py` in python, the baml bindings do not represent reality. E.g. `Pdf` from `baml_py` has bindings:\n\n```python\nclass BamlPdfPy:\n    # other bindings not important\n    def from_base64(media_type: str, base64: str) -> BamlPdfPy: ...\n```\n\nBased on these bindings, we should do:\n```python\nfrom baml_py import Pdf\n\nb64_str = ...\nPdf.from_base64(\"application/pdf\", b64_str)\n```\n\nbut if we do this, we get:\n\n```stderr\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTypeError                                 Traceback (most recent call last)\nCell In[5], [line 11](vscode-notebook-cell:?execution_count=5&line=11)\n      9 cr = ClientRegistry()\n     10 cr.set_primary(\"CustomGPT4oMini\")\n---> [11](vscode-notebook-cell:?execution_count=5&line=11) pdf = Pdf.from_base64(\"application/pdf\", base64.b64encode(pdf_bytes).decode())\n     13 b.ProcessInvoice(pdf, {\"client_registry\": cr})\n\nTypeError: BamlPdfPy.from_base64() takes 1 positional arguments but 2 were given\n```\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\n1. Install BAML 0.205.0\n2. Have a python LSP installed\n3. Run baml in a python project\n4. Based on bindings run\n\n```python\nfrom baml_py import Pdf\n\nb64_str = ...\nPdf.from_base64(\"application/pdf\", b64_str)\n```\n\n```stderr\n---------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTypeError                                 Traceback (most recent call last)\nCell In[5], [line 11](vscode-notebook-cell:?execution_count=5&line=11)\n      9 cr = ClientRegistry()\n     10 cr.set_primary(\"CustomGPT4oMini\")\n---> [11](vscode-notebook-cell:?execution_count=5&line=11) pdf = Pdf.from_base64(\"application/pdf\", base64.b64encode(pdf_bytes).decode())\n     13 b.ProcessInvoice(pdf, {\"client_registry\": cr})\n\nTypeError: BamlPdfPy.from_base64() takes 1 positional arguments but 2 were given\n```\n\n5. Following the error, remove the \"application/pdf\"\n\n```python\nfrom baml_py import Pdf\n\nb64_str = ...\nPdf.from_base64(b64_str)\n```\n\n6. See how the LSP complains, because the bindings don''t follow the actual function definintion\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.205.0\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nPython\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2296', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2296", "title": "export declare duplicated in TypeScript type definitions", "body": "Today I used [patch-package](https://github.com/ds300/patch-package) to patch `@boundaryml/baml@0.204.0` for the project I''m working on.\n\nHere is the diff that solved my problem:\n\n```diff\ndiff --git a/node_modules/@boundaryml/baml/native.d.ts b/node_modules/@boundaryml/baml/native.d.ts\nindex 07a0ebd..3aa6b6d 100644\n--- a/node_modules/@boundaryml/baml/native.d.ts\n+++ b/node_modules/@boundaryml/baml/native.d.ts\n@@ -253,11 +253,11 @@ export interface BamlLogEvent {\n   startTime: string\n }\n \n-export declare export declare function get_version(): string\n+export declare function get_version(): string\n \n-export declare export declare function getLogLevel(): string\n+export declare function getLogLevel(): string\n \n-export declare export declare function invoke_runtime_cli(params: Array<string>): number\n+export declare function invoke_runtime_cli(params: Array<string>): number\n \n export interface LogEventMetadata {\n   eventId: string\n@@ -265,9 +265,9 @@ export interface LogEventMetadata {\n   rootEventId: string\n }\n \n-export declare export declare function setLogJsonMode(useJson: boolean): void\n+export declare function setLogJsonMode(useJson: boolean): void\n \n-export declare export declare function setLogLevel(level: string): void\n+export declare function setLogLevel(level: string): void\n \n-export declare export declare function setLogMaxChunkLength(length: number): void\n+export declare function setLogMaxChunkLength(length: number): void\n \n```\n\n<em>This issue body was [partially generated by patch-package](https://github.com/ds300/patch-package/issues/296).</em>", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2273', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2273", "title": "[bug] REST API Client Generation failure - directory contains files that BAML did not generate", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nBAML development server started with `npx @boundaryml/baml dev`\n\nInitial generation of `baml_client` output directory works successfully. When a file is changed and saved, an error occurs during regeneration.\n\nError Message:\n```text\n2025-08-08T09:49:22.102 [BAML INFO] Starting BAML development server on port 2024\n2025-08-08T09:49:22.106 [BAML INFO] Wrote 3 files to baml_client\n2025-08-08T09:49:22.106 [BAML INFO] Running \"openapi-generator generate -i openapi.yaml -g go -o . --additional-properties enumClassPrefix=true,isGoSubmodule=true,packageName=baml_client,withGoMod=false\" in baml_client\n2025-08-08T09:49:22.820 [BAML INFO] Generated 1 baml_client: ../baml_client\n2025-08-08T09:49:22.821 [BAML INFO] BAML-over-HTTP listening on port 2024, serving from ./baml_src\n\nTip: test that the server is up using `curl http://localhost:2024/_debug/ping`\n\n(You may need to replace \"localhost\" with the container hostname as appropriate.)\n2025-08-08T09:49:44.471 [BAML ERROR] Error generating clients: Client generation failed\n\nCaused by:\n    output directory contains at least 5 files that BAML did not generate\n\n    Please remove all files not generated by BAML and re-run codegen.\n\n    Files:\n      - baml_client/model_baml_pdf_base64.go\n      - baml_client/model_baml_video_url.go\n      - baml_client/model_extract_resume_request.go\n      - baml_client/model_resume.go\n      - baml_client/test/api_default_test.go\n2025-08-08T09:49:44.471 [BAML INFO] Reloaded runtime in 3ms (2 files changed)\n```\n\nThese are all files that were created during the initial `baml_client` directory .\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nFollow the instructions [here](https://docs.boundaryml.com/guide/installation-language/rest-api-other-languages) to install and start the development server.\n\nRunning `npx @boundaryml/baml dev --preview` this command causes an error, so I removed the `--preview` to start the development server.\n\nSave a change to a `.baml` file\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.204\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nGo\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nGoogle\n\n### LLM Model\n\ngemini-2.5-flash-lite\n\n### Operating System\n\nmacOS\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nVS Code", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug"]}'::jsonb, 'bug'),
('kind:gh-2915', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2915", "title": "[bug] Defining @skip and @@dynamic together throws error", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nWhen I use @ skip and @@ dynamic together in a Class defintion I get the following error: \n\n```bash\n2026-01-05T12:42:24.873 [BAML ERROR] Function ExtractProduct:\n    Operation cancelled..\n\nthread ''<unnamed>'' panicked at /home/<user>/work/baml/baml/engine/baml-lib/jsonish/src/deserializer/semantic_streaming.rs:130:26:\nThis field is guaranteed to be in the field set\nstack backtrace:\n   0: __rustc::rust_begin_unwind\n   1: core::panicking::panic_fmt\n   2: core::option::expect_failed\n   3: jsonish::deserializer::semantic_streaming::process_node\n   4: jsonish::deserializer::semantic_streaming::process_node\n   5: baml_runtime::internal::prompt_renderer::PromptRenderer::parse\n   6: baml_runtime::internal::llm_client::orchestrator::call::orchestrate::{{closure}}::{{closure}}\n   7: <core::pin::Pin<P> as core::future::future::Future>::poll\n   8: tokio::runtime::park::CachedParkThread::block_on::{{closure}}\n   9: baml_runtime::async_vm_runtime::BamlAsyncVmRuntime::call_function_sync\n  10: baml_py::runtime::BamlRuntime::call_function_sync\n  11: baml_py::runtime::BamlRuntime::__pymethod_call_function_sync__\n  12: pyo3::impl_::trampoline::trampoline\n  13: baml_py::runtime::<impl pyo3::impl_::pyclass::PyMethods<baml_py::runtime::BamlRuntime> for pyo3::impl_::pyclass::PyClassImplCollector<baml_py::runtime::BamlRuntime>>::py_methods::ITEMS::trampoline\n  14: cfunction_call\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Objects/methodobject.c:539:18\n  15: _PyObject_MakeTpCall\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Objects/call.c:242:18\n  16: _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Python/generated_cases.c.h:1850:23\n  17: _PyEval_EvalFrame\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Include/internal/pycore_ceval.h:121:16\n  18: _PyEval_Vector\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Python/ceval.c:1820:12\n  19: _PyFunction_Vectorcall\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Objects/call.c:413:16\n  20: _PyObject_VectorcallTstate\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Include/internal/pycore_call.h:168:11\n  21: method_vectorcall\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Objects/classobject.c:71\n  22: thread_run\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Modules/_threadmodule.c:343:0\n  23: pythread_wrapper\n             at /usr/local/src/conda/python-3.13.11/Python/thread_pthread.h:242:0\n  24: <unknown>\n  25: <unknown>\nnote: S", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2883', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2883", "title": "[bug] Go: `CallFunction...` BAML runtime methods leak goroutines", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nThe generated `parse` and `parse_stream` methods in the Golang client call `bamlRuntime.CallFunctionParse` with a `context.Background()`, and `CallFunctionParse` unconditionally spawns a goroutine that waits for the context to be done ([link](https://github.com/BoundaryML/baml/blob/629c14e88171f89240713598a156f0a7712d6b6f/engine/language_client_go/pkg/runtime.go#L129)), but since nothing else can make that goroutine stop, and `context.Background()` will never complete, the goroutine leaks.\n\nThis pattern of \"wait for context to be done\" goroutines appears in `bamlRuntime.CallFunction` as well, but at least there the context is user-controlled, so the goroutine leak is \"avoidable\", but definitely still realistic, especially considering the fact that BAML docs encourage the usage of `context.Background()` through [the official examples](https://docs.boundaryml.com/guide/installation-language/go#use-a-baml-function-in-go).\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\n1. Set up a Go BAML project with some goroutine profiling mechanism like pprof\n2. Run some BAML functions\n3. Observe the multiple leaked goroutines via your profiling mechanism of choice, e.g.:\n\n<img width=\"969\" height=\"885\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/df79d268-f810-418d-9897-09c25f1c9562\" />\n\n### BAML Version\n\nIdentified on 0.214.0, evidently present in latest as well judging by the code\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nGo\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2858', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2858", "title": "[bug] embedded baml playground is broken", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nhttps://docs.boundaryml.com/guide/baml-basics/testing-functions#try-it-press-run-test-below-1\n\nsays \"Try it! Press ‘Run Test’ below!\"\n\nBut I don''t see any \"run test\" to press here, and the \"loading BAML runtime\" is just hanging. Tried Chrome and Firefox:\n\n<img width=\"916\" height=\"701\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e06894d9-f4e8-4bec-9fe1-98d80f0274b3\" />\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nGo to https://docs.boundaryml.com/guide/baml-basics/testing-functions#try-it-press-run-test-below-1\n\n### BAML Version\n\nn/a, docs website\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nNone\n\n### LLM Model\n\n_No response_\n\n### Operating System\n\nNone\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2795', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2795", "title": "[feat] Baml plugin work with JetBrains 2025.3", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Problem Statement / Use Case\n\nBaml plugin can''t work with JetBrains 2025.3\n\n<img width=\"339\" height=\"204\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/5f1ec26f-2e8f-45ea-b93b-3fa42f72de00\" />\n\nThanks a lot if anyone can take the effort to upgrade it.\n \n\n\n### Proposed Solution\n\nUpdate JetBrains compatible version and test it.\n\n### Alternative Solutions\n\n_No response_\n\n### Additional Context\n\n_No response_", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2791', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2791", "title": "Ruby warning about Baml::StreamState being already initialized and previous definition of StreamState existing", "body": "We''re using the library on our Ruby on Rails application, and we see these 2 warnings everywhere, anytime we run the app, or any time we run unit tests.\n\nIs there a way to address this issue and prevent the warnings from showing up?\n\n```\n.../ruby/3.4.7/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/baml-0.90.2-arm64-darwin/lib/baml.rb:23: warning: already initialized constant Baml::StreamState\n.../ruby/3.4.7/lib/ruby/gems/3.4.0/gems/baml-0.90.2-arm64-darwin/lib/stream.rb:18: warning: previous definition of StreamState was here\n```", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2782', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2782", "title": "[bug] TS: error 400 (cant support image/pdf) when test using pdf in an array", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nModel (gemini) return 400 when the param of test is an array of file\n\nUnspecified error code: 400 Request failed with status code: 400 Bad Request, \n    {\n      \"error\": {\n        \"code\": 400,\n        \"message\": \"Unsupported MIME type: image/pdf\",\n        \"status\": \"INVALID_ARGUMENT\"\n      }\n    }\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\nMake a test like this\n\ntest ExtractContractStructureTest {\n  functions [ExtractContractStructure]\n  args {\n    contract_files [\n      {\n        file \"../files/test.pdf\"\n      }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n\non which contract files have type: (pdf | image)[]\n\n\nAnd then it will return \n\nUnspecified error code: 400 Request failed with status code: 400 Bad Request, \n    {\n      \"error\": {\n        \"code\": 400,\n        \"message\": \"Unsupported MIME type: image/pdf\",\n        \"status\": \"INVALID_ARGUMENT\"\n      }\n    }\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.214.0\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nNode.js\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nGoogle\n\n### LLM Model\n\ngemini-2.5-flash-lite\n\n### Operating System\n\nmacOS\n\n### Browser\n\nSafari\n\n### Code Editor\n\nVS Code", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:gh-2747', 'kind', '{"id": "gh-2747", "title": "[bug] Logs are written to stdout instead of stderr", "body": "### Product\n\nBAML\n\n### Describe the bug\n\nUsing the current latest version of baml-py (0.214.0) logs are written to stdout by default.\n\nThey should be written to stderr.\n\n### Reproduction Steps\n\n```\nimport os\nos.environ[\"BAML_LOG\"] = \"INFO\"\n\n# interact with LLM via baml\n...\n```\n```\n$ python prog.py 1>stdout.log 2>stderr.log\n$ cat stdout.log\n```\n\n### BAML Version\n\n0.214.0\n\n### Language/Framework\n\nPython\n\n### LLM Provider\n\nOpenAI\n\n### LLM Model\n\nany-model\n\n### Operating System\n\nmacOS\n\n### Browser\n\nNone\n\n### Code Editor\n\nNone", "kind": "bug", "labels": ["bug (small)"]}'::jsonb, 'bug (small)'),
('kind:both-1', 'kind', '{"id": "both-1", "title": "baml.sap.parse drops array entries with unquoted keys; also want a strict mode", "body": "On 0.19.1-nightly.20260911.a, `baml.sap.parse<Routes>(`{routes:[{id:\"1\"}]}`)` returns `routes: []` while the spaced form `{ routes: [ { id: \"1\" } ] }` returns one entry. That is a bug: equivalent whitespace variants must parse the same.\n\nSeparately, we would like a strict option, e.g. `baml.sap.parse<T>(text, strict = true)`, that raises on any coercion (unquoted keys, trailing commas, missing fields) instead of guessing, so persisted telemetry is never silently altered. Today there is no way to opt out of the permissive behaviour.", "kind": "both", "labels": ["synthetic"]}'::jsonb, 'synthetic'),
('kind:both-2', 'kind', '{"id": "both-2", "title": "bigint JSON roundtrip fails, and there is no bigint.to_string(radix)", "body": "`baml.json.from_string<Record>(baml.json.to_string(Record { elapsed_ms: 12n }))` throws `bigint JSON decoding not yet implemented` on 0.19.1-nightly.20260911.a. The encoder accepts bigint fields so the decoder must too.\n\nWhile here: please add `bigint.to_string(radix: int)` so we can print hex ids; `to_string()` only does base 10 and we hand-roll the conversion.", "kind": "both", "labels": ["synthetic"]}'::jsonb, 'synthetic'),
('kind:both-3', 'kind', '{"id": "both-3", "title": "`baml fmt` reorders class fields (wrong) and should get a --check flag", "body": "Running `baml fmt main.baml` on a class with fields `b: int, a: int` rewrites them as `a: int, b: int` on 0.19.1-nightly. Field order is significant for our generated clients, so this is a formatter bug.\n\nWe also want `baml fmt --check` that exits non-zero when files would change, for CI, like rustfmt --check.", "kind": "both", "labels": ["synthetic"]}'::jsonb, 'synthetic'),
('kind:both-4', 'kind', '{"id": "both-4", "title": "Streaming partial parser truncates \\uXXXX escapes; request: expose the partial parser directly", "body": "With `baml test` on 0.19.1-nightly, feeding `{\"s\": \"\\u00e9` to the partial parser yields `\\u00` and then `é` only after the closing quote arrives; the fourth hex digit is dropped mid-stream. Expected: either the full escape or nothing until it is complete.\n\nFeature: expose `baml.json.parse_partial<T>(chunk)` publicly so host code can drive incremental parsing without going through an LLM function.", "kind": "both", "labels": ["synthetic"]}'::jsonb, 'synthetic'),
